<p>Apple&#8217;s 27.3 Developer <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/safari-update-brings-vertical-tabs-adaptive-dark-mode-ai-integration/">betas added some major Siri AI features to Safari</a>, but that&#8217;s not all. There&#8217;s also the <a href="https://developer.apple.com/news/?id=gdk2umrf">Siri AI Developer Preview</a>, which Federico Viticci <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/siri-ais-evolution-is-both-surprising-and-obvious/">analyzed in detail</a>, finding it to be a sign that Apple really is executing fast on its AI development game plan:</p>
<blockquote><p>
  You don’t roll out this kind of expansion two months after the launch of iOS 27 unless you truly have a flexible orchestration layer and modern harness that can make the assistant more useful simply by virtue of giving it more tools.</p>
<p>  It’s been a slow burn for Siri AI over the past two months, especially since it was always a bit unclear what would work and what would break when using it inside apps. I’m curious to test the updated architecture over the next “few months” (<a href="https://developer.apple.com/news/?id=gdk2umrf">per Apple</a>), and I’m now very confident about the fact that Apple is building a Siri Agent for iOS 28.
</p></blockquote>
<p>Apple lost two years by fumbling the Apple Intelligence roll-out. It&#8217;s good to see that it still has a serious sense of urgency and isn&#8217;t hibernating until WWDC 2027.</p>
